tive. One-off effect: noisy CPS occupational allocation argues against extrapolating the full decline. Policy mechanism: no discrete policy change identified, while weak financial-activities payroll evidence supports only a small negative adjustment."},{"kind":"math","text":"Prior/update/interval: persistence model prior = 9.720 million; historical sample = 10.205, 9.857, and 9.720 million. Successive observed changes are -0.348 and -0.137 million, giving RMS sigma = sqrt((0.348^2 + 0.137^2)/2) = 0.264 million. Apply a -0.030 million momentum/financial-activities adjustment and no separate one-off or policy adjustment, yielding 9.690 million. The 80% half-width is approximately 1.28*sigma = 1.28*0.264 = 0.338 million, rounded to 0.34, implying 9.35 to 10.03 million."},{"kind":"text","text":"Upside risk is a rebound in sampled professional hiring or July occupational reclassification, which would land above 10.03 million. Downside risk is continued finance-sector weakness combined with adverse CPS sampling, which would land below 9.35 million and outside the interval."},{"kind":"forecast","point":9.69,"ciLow":9.35,"ciHigh":10.03}]}
